# Notes about lvalue and rvalue

## lvalue
Definition: A value that has a name, whose address can be taken, and which persists after the expression ends.
Examples:
```c++
int a = 10; //a is an lvalue
int b = a + 1; //b is an lvalue
int* p = &a; //valid, a is an lvalue
```


## rvalue
Definition: A value that lacks a persistent identity and is typically temporary.
Examples:
```c++
int a = 10; //10 is an rvalue
int b = a + 1; //a + 1 is an rvalue
// int* p2 = &(a + 1); //invalid, a + 1 is an rvalue
```

## reference

### Lvalue reference *T&*
Definition: *T&* can only bind to an lvalue; it cannot directly bind to an rvalue.
Examples:
```c++
int a = 5;
int& r1 = a;      // valid, a is an lvalue
// int& r2 = 10;  // invalid, 10 is an rvalue(Literal)
// int& r3 = a + 1; // invalid, a + 1 is an rvalue
```

### Const lvalue reference *const T&*
Definition: *const T&* can bind to both lvalues ​​and rvalues.
Examples:
```c++
int a = 5;
const int& r1 = a;     // valid, a is an lvalue
const int& r2 = 10;    // valid, 10 is an rvalue(Literal)
const int& r3 = a + 1; // valid, a + 1 is an rvalue
```

### Rvalue reference *T&&*
Definition: *T&&* can only bind to rvalues; it cannot directly bind to a lvalue.
Examples:
```c++
int a = 5;
int&& r1 = 10;        // valid
int&& r2 = a + 1;     // valid
// int&& r3 = a;      // invalid，a is an lvalue
int&& r4 = std::move(a); // valid, std::move converts an lvalue to an rvalue
```
